  <h2>Store first, build second</h2>
  <p>Each run fetches both feeds, checks every row against a contract, and commits the snapshot to an orphan <code>data</code> branch before anything downstream can fail. Only then does <code>dbt build</code> run. If dbt or the export fails, the readings are already stored. A recovery job redeploys the last good export with a status file naming the failing step, and opens one "pipeline failing" issue. The next good run closes it.</p>
  <p>Error-severity tests check only the current run. Each one has a warning copy over all of history, so one bad old snapshot can't block every later run.</p>

  <h2>Honest about the schedule</h2>
  <p>The pipeline runs on GitHub Actions cron, which is best-effort. The schedule asks for two runs an hour; on 2026-10-28 only 3 of about 14 hourly runs fired, and the median gap in the first weeks was about three hours. The page doesn't hide this: it shows a run strip and the age of every reading.</p>

  <h2>What the feeds actually do</h2>
  <p>Each of these was seen in the live feeds and is pinned by a test:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>CBP stamps an update with the coming hour: fetched at 15:53 EDT, several ports said <code>At 4:00 pm EDT</code>. Times up to a day ahead are kept and warned about. Further ahead fails the build.</li>
    <li>CBP writes noon as <code>At Noon EDT</code> and midnight as <code>At Midnight</code>. The midnight form broke two scheduled runs before the parser learned it.</li>
    <li>A late-evening CBP time fetched after local midnight belongs to the day before.</li>
    <li>The two sides of a crossing can be in different time zones, so every crossing has a zone per side.</li>
    <li>Agencies sometimes change a value under an unchanged update time (CBP did, 2 of 4,124 updates by 2026-11-14). The later value is kept and marked revised.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Rules with tests behind them</h2>
  <p>A row with a category outside the contract, a delay outside 0–720 minutes, or an update time that doesn't parse stays in the snapshots but is left out of every mart. Nothing is clipped or deleted. A newer "Update Pending" or "Lanes Closed" reading replaces an older number instead of carrying it over. Each of these is a decision, and each has a test.</p>

  <h2>The page</h2>
  <p>The page reads a manifest, a status file and a summary. DuckDB-Wasm loads only when a visitor opens the SQL console. If the live files can't be loaded or fail the contract, the page shows a saved snapshot, labelled as one, and the console runs on that.</p>

